  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Gusty winds from the west this morning into \nearly afternoon could lead to isolated power outages mainly in \nfar Northern Maine.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nMuch drier air is quickly moving in, but with that is a strong\npressure gradient. Winds aloft are presently increasing, and\nthink that once the sun rises and the clouds clear out, we could\nsee these strong winds just off the surface get down to the\nsurface. Winds will be a fair amount stronger in the north than\ntoward the coast, and have issued a wind advisory for areas from\nMars Hill north. Gusts as high as about 45 mph could lead to\nsome branches and perhaps a few trees being downed, which would \nlead to a few power outages. Winds of this magnitude tend to \nlead to impacts on trees when you have leaves on the trees and \nwet soils like we have right now. Winds will still be gusty \nDowneast (to about 30 mph), but not as bad as in the north. \nWinds will begin to decrease early this afternoon and will be \nquite a bit lighter toward sunset today.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Heat and humidity builds through the end of the\nweek, peaking Friday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nAfter a mild day today, temperatures are expected to rise as\nhigh pressure moves over our area, driving a SW flow that should\nhelp warmer air move into the region. Temperatures are expected\nto be 5 to 10 degrees above normal and may approach 90 degrees \nwhich in combination with relatively high humidity should favor \nsome heat risk concerns. The hottest day over the North should \nbe Wednesday, with Thursday and Friday likely the hottest over \nthe Bangor region and Downeast Maine. The coast is likely to \nbenefit from cooler conditions as onshore flow should bring \nmarine air inland.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Scattered showers and thunderstorms late this \nweek. Some severe storms are possible. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3 DESCRIPTION...\nA cold front is expected to move through the area on Friday and\nmove out Saturday morning as another wave of low pressure moves\nin from the west. This is likely to drive thunderstorm\ndevelopment Friday and Saturday. The greatest risk Friday is \nover the North and further south on Saturday. Current long \nrange model guidance is showing the potential for up to 1500 \nJ/kg of instability with 20 to 30 kts of deep layer shear which \nin combination with steep low-level lapse rates driven by warm \nsurface temperatures could support storms capable of a few \nstrong wind gusts Fri/Sat. Still some uncertainty in severe \npotential depending on frontal timing and given that AI guidance\nis not very bullish on severe potential. There is slightly \ngreater confidence that any storms on Friday could produce heavy\nrainfall as atmospheric moisture is expected to be well above \naverage for this time of year and the atmospheric thermodynamic \nprofile appears favorable for efficient rainfall. Less confident\non the flash flooding threat as sufficiently strong upper-level\nwinds should keep storms from staying over the same area for \ntoo long. Less atmospheric moisture expected Saturday so the \nheavy rainfall threat is reduced.",
